Spiral Safe Veil quorum scaffold for AWS Nitro Enclaves
=======================================================

Status: fail-closed enrollment and same-image configuration scaffold, not a
production Nitro quorum. It replaces the archived Nitriding build dependency
with pinned [Veil](https://github.com/Amnesic-Systems/veil), but Veil is not a
drop-in Nitriding replacement and it does not solve durable storage, cross-host
routing, or secret delivery for Vault.

Implemented contract
--------------------

One Linux/amd64 container image and the EIF built from it can be used for either
the first Vault node or a joiner. Node-specific state is not a Docker build
argument. A short-lived schema-v1 manifest selects `bootstrap` or `join` at run
time and records these release/runtime inputs:

* the operator-declared OCI image digest, EIF SHA-384, Spiral Safe source
  revision, and exact Veil revision;
* a unique Vault Raft `node_id`, advertised API and cluster addresses, and an
  odd minimum voter count of at least three;
* a shared AWS KMS key identifier for auto-unseal, without embedding AWS
  credentials;
* node/server, operator CA, and join-client TLS file paths, never key contents;
* one or more HTTPS `retry_join` leaders with CA, client certificate, client
  key, and `leader_tls_servername` inputs;
* explicit names for the operator-provided durable-storage bridge and
  cross-host L4 route; and
* WebAuthn RP configuration consumed by the Vault plugin.

`config-renderer/` strictly decodes that manifest, rejects unknown fields,
placeholders, expired admission windows longer than 30 minutes, unsafe paths,
group/other-writable TLS trust files, weak private-key permissions, duplicate
leaders, wrong Veil tunnel binds, and missing security requirements. It
atomically renders mode-0600 Vault HCL and a validated plugin environment file.
The manifest template is intentionally invalid until every `REPLACE_` and
`REQUIRED_` value is supplied.

The renderer validates only the syntax and non-placeholder value of
`image.digest`. Neither Nitro attestation nor the current enrollment script can
recover and compare an OCI registry digest from a running EIF, so this field is
release inventory, not a cryptographic admission claim. Bind it to signed build
provenance in the external delivery system before treating it as authoritative.
The current hardware identity inputs are the independently obtained EIF SHA-384
and the PCR/nonce/TLS/source checks performed by `veil-verify`.

Network boundary proven from pinned Veil source
-----------------------------------------------

Veil revision `2b8c06ca651e09b21832f6fc4ae2605371386f76` creates one VSOCK
connection carrying a full IP TUN. The EC2 parent is `10.0.0.1` and its enclave
is `10.0.0.2`, so one EIF can listen on several TCP ports over that tunnel.

| Port | Purpose | Authentication boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `8443` | Veil `/veil`, `/veil/config`, and `/veil/attestation` | nonce-bound Nitro attestation and attested ephemeral TLS key |
| `18200` | Vault HTTPS API on `10.0.0.2` | operator/client CA mTLS plus normal Vault authorization |
| `18201` | Vault Raft cluster transport on `10.0.0.2` | Vault cluster TLS; never public ingress |
| `8200` | enclave-loopback Vault listener | enclave-local only |

The Veil HTTPS listener is intentionally attestation-only in this scaffold. Its
single reverse-proxy facility cannot require the independent client certificate
used by the Vault API, so exposing Vault through it would bypass the manifest's
mTLS admission boundary.

The identical image bakes `spiral-safe-attestation.invalid` as Veil's shared
certificate name. This is a measured sentinel, not a routable per-node FQDN and
not a browser-compatible PKI identity. Pinned Veil's verifier deliberately
connects with ordinary certificate/hostname verification disabled because the
certificate is authenticated by attestation instead. It hashes the leaf
certificate actually presented by that connection and requires the exact hash
inside the fresh, nonce-bound attestation document before comparing PCRs. Thus
each manifest's `node.veilAddress` is only the operator-routed address used to
reach that node; distinct addresses or ports can reach distinct nodes while all
nodes retain the same measured EIF and shared sentinel certificate name.

Only the official pinned `veil-verify` flow may consume port 8443. Do not treat
the `.invalid` certificate as a normal CA identity, add it to a trust store, or
replace verification with a generic `curl -k` check. If a normal hostname-valid
Veil frontend is required, per-node run-time FQDN delivery before Veil starts is
an additional unresolved blocker because Nitro CLI cannot inject it. This
scaffold intentionally does not claim that mode.

Veil does not provide a cross-host router or a multi-listener L4 publishing
configuration. Moreover, every host/enclave pair reuses `10.0.0.1/10.0.0.2`.
An operator must therefore supply a reviewed private overlay or per-host
forwarder that maps each node's unique API and cluster addresses to its local
TUN. Security groups must expose `18200` only to authorized operators/services
and `18201` only to admitted peers. The string in `externalL4Route` records the
approved implementation; the renderer does not create it.

Pinned source, images, and licensing
------------------------------------

The Docker build fetches and verifies Veil v0.5.1 at commit
`2b8c06ca651e09b21832f6fc4ae2605371386f76`. Go 1.26 and Vault 1.21.4 are
digest-pinned multi-platform images. The build runs the node-renderer tests and
builds the current Spiral Safe plugin before producing the final image. The
image records the Veil source and manifest schema as OCI labels.

Veil is GPL-3.0 licensed. Distributing an image containing its binaries can
create notice and corresponding-source obligations. Keep the exact pin, Veil
license at `/licenses/veil-GPL-3.0.txt`, reproducible build instructions, and a
durable source offer with every distributed image. Obtain legal review before
external distribution. Vault 1.21.4 is BUSL-1.1 and Spiral Safe is Apache-2.0;
review and satisfy all applicable license terms. OCI labels are metadata, not a
substitute for notices or source delivery.

Build and local topology proof
------------------------------

From `services/nitro`:

    make image
    make tools
    make test

`make image` produces an ordinary Linux container archive and does not launch
an enclave. `make tools` exports the pinned Linux/amd64 `veil-proxy`,
`veil-verify`, and `spiral-node-config` binaries to ignored `.tools/`.
`make test` needs Go, `jq`, and ShellCheck on the host.

The two-node simulation renders a bootstrap manifest and a join manifest with
the same image identity and KMS key, distinct node IDs and temporary Raft
directories, a separate API/cluster bind, and mTLS `retry_join`:

    make simulate

This is only topology/configuration proof. Temporary host directories are not
Nitro persistence, and the simulation does not claim a live Vault quorum, KMS,
cross-host routing, or real attestation.

EIF and admission workflow
--------------------------

EIF creation and launch require a Linux/amd64 enclave-enabled EC2 parent with
the Nitro Enclaves CLI. No Make target terminates an enclave or performs an
unscoped lifecycle operation.

1. Build one release image and EIF from a clean pinned checkout:

       make image eif tools

2. Copy `same-image-manifest.template.json` outside the source tree. Fill the
   exact image/EIF/source pins, a unique node ID, role, routable node addresses,
   TLS paths, shared KMS key ID, WebAuthn values, and an admission window of at
   most 30 minutes. A bootstrap manifest has no leaders or join TLS fields; a
   joiner has at least one mTLS `retry_join` leader.

3. Validate before launch:

       NODE_MANIFEST=/secure/node-a.json CHECK_FILES=false make validate-config

4. Launch the named EIF and parent Veil proxy:

       NODE_MANIFEST=/secure/node-a.json make run

   Nitro CLI cannot inject environment variables or files into a running EIF.
   The image therefore starts Veil first for attestation while Vault waits for
   `/run/spiral-safe/node-manifest.json`. The current scaffold deliberately has
   no generic upload endpoint.

5. From a disposable clean checkout at the manifest's exact source revision,
   verify the running enclave. `veil-verify` rebuilds the pinned Dockerfile,
   compares PCRs, verifies a fresh nonce, and binds the TLS certificate to the
   attestation document:

       NODE_MANIFEST=/secure/node-a.json \
       SOURCE_DIR=/secure/clean-services-checkout \
       make verify-admission

   Verification fails if the renderer, EIF hash, source revision, exact Veil
   pin, clean-tree check, official verifier, or expected revision check fails.
   A verifier-created `enclave.tar` is removed after the attempt.

6. Configure an absolute executable `SPIRAL_ENROLLMENT_HOOK` backed by the
   operator's admission service, then run `make admit`. The hook is called only
   after successful Veil verification and receives the verified node ID and EIF
   SHA-384 as environment metadata. Its stdout must be exactly one short-lived
   `file:/...` or `secret-ref://...` reference of at most 512 characters:

       SPIRAL_ENROLLMENT_HOOK=/opt/spiral/bin/issue-enrollment \
       NODE_MANIFEST=/secure/node-a.json \
       SOURCE_DIR=/secure/clean-services-checkout \
       make admit

   The command prints only that reference, never the manifest contents, TLS
   private keys, AWS credentials, Vault token, recovery material, or bundle.
   The external admission system must bind the reference to the verified EIF,
   expire it, authorize one node ID, issue least-privilege TLS/KMS identity, and
   deliver the manifest plus referenced files over an independently reviewed
   attestation-bound channel. That delivery agent is not implemented by Veil or
   this repository and is a production blocker.

7. After Vault is initialized/unsealed and a joiner has converged, use a
   short-lived operator token and separate client certificate to prove the
   exact voter and advertised cluster address, an odd voter count at or above
   the minimum, and healthy Autopilot failure tolerance for that actual count:

       VAULT_CACERT=/secure/operator-ca.crt \
       VAULT_CLIENT_CERT=/secure/operator.crt \
       VAULT_CLIENT_KEY=/secure/operator.key \
       VAULT_TLS_SERVER_NAME=vault.internal \
       VAULT_TOKEN=REDACTED \
       NODE_MANIFEST=/secure/node-a.json \
       make check-membership

The membership check uses `vault status`, `vault operator raft list-peers`, and
`vault operator raft autopilot state`. It never prints the token.

Persistence and auto-unseal boundary
------------------------------------

Nitro Enclaves have no durable local block storage. `/vault/data` disappears
with the enclave. `durableStorageBridge` is a required declaration, not an
implementation. A production voter needs an attested external bridge with
POSIX-compatible durability, ordering and fsync behavior, encryption and
rollback protection, crash recovery, fencing, per-node isolation, snapshot and
restore tests, and a threat model showing the parent cannot fork or rewind Raft
state. An object-store copy loop is not a safe substitute.

All nodes must refer to the same approved auto-unseal key, but the `awskms`
stanza alone does not prove that KMS access is bound to Nitro measurements.
Never embed IAM access keys or KMS credentials. The external admission/delivery
system must provide an attestation-bound workload identity or a narrowly scoped
KMS proxy and must demonstrate failover and recovery. Until both this identity
path and durable storage exist, do not admit these enclaves as production Raft
voters.

Removal, rollback, and recovery
-------------------------------

Admission failure is fail-closed: do not release a bundle, identity, or TLS
certificate. Before a candidate becomes a voter, revoke its short-lived bundle
reference and issued identity, then discard its candidate storage.

After a node is a voter, do not simply terminate it or reuse its data directory.
First confirm quorum and Autopilot health from a different authorized node,
remove the exact peer with the reviewed `vault operator raft remove-peer`
procedure, confirm the remaining peer list and failure tolerance, revoke its TLS
and KMS authorization, remove its private routes, and only then destroy or
quarantine its durable state. Preserve an odd healthy voter count. A replacement
gets a new unique node ID and a fresh admission; old Raft data must not be
silently attached to another identity.

Backups require encrypted, authenticated Raft snapshots in off-host storage and
regular restore/quorum-loss exercises. The external durability layer must also
define anti-rollback counters and disaster recovery; neither Veil nor this
scaffold supplies them.

Unresolved release gates
------------------------

The following are intentionally not claimed or emulated:

* real Nitro PCR/nonce/TLS verification on enclave-enabled EC2 hardware;
* an attested manifest/secret receiver inside the EIF;
* durable, rollback-protected enclave storage for voting Raft members;
* cross-host routing for the reused Veil TUN subnet;
* attestation-bound shared auto-unseal identity and KMS policy;
* a live three-node Vault quorum, rolling upgrades, snapshots/restores, failure
  injection, and quorum-loss recovery; and
* end-to-end service signing through the enclave topology.

